  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Let $B=[\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & x-40 \\\\ 1 & -x\\end{aligned}]$\nbe a singular matrix. **In ascending order**, the values of $x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(2x+1)\\cdot (-x)-(x-40)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}-x-x+40 \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}-2x+40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-2x+40 & =0 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+x-20) & =0 \\\\ -2(x-4)(x+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =4,-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $x$ are\n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=4.\n$$",
